  "local_bulletin": {
    "source_name": "Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD)",
    "text": "Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) — forecast for Kisumu\nValid for 2026-09-16.\nMax 31.0C / Min 18.0C.\nTonight: Partly cloudy\nMorning: Sunny intervals\nAfternoon: Sunny intervals, light showers likely over few places.\nNational outlook mentions this area:\n  - Rainfall expected in some parts of Kericho, Kakamega, Vihiga, Nandi, Kisumu, Vihiga, Baringo, Bomet, Homa Bay, Nakuru,",
    "fetched_at_utc": "2026-09-16T03:03:18.229492Z"
  },
  "yesterday_verification_summary": "Across yesterday's verification checks for September 15th, all models successfully verified precipitation at Day+0, with ECMWF and Best Match demonstrating superior temperature and rain calibration. Day+3 checks for September 12th saw most models verify the rain event, though Best Match recorded a false call while surface winds were widely under-forecasted. Day+7 verification for September 8th showed accurate precipitation calls across GFS, ECMWF, and Best Match, despite minor temperature and wind offsets.",
  "narrative_markdown": "## Overview\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected this evening and tonight, with thunderstorm activity peaking around 18:00. Temperatures and winds much the same through Saturday, with rain becoming more likely.\n\n## Today's Forecast\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected this evening and overnight, with rain onset anticipated between 18:00 and 21:00. Tonight's precipitation is projected at 3.1 mm (0.12 in) with an 88% probability of rain. Peak surface wind gusts ashore will reach 42 km/h (23 kt). Daytime high temperatures reached 31°C / 88°F earlier, and temperatures will cool to an overnight low of 18°C / 65°F. No current ground AQI data is available; the last actual reading was US AQI 72 at Ochieng' Avenue, Kisumu Central, 3 hours ago. CAMS model guidance indicates air quality remains Moderate (US AQI 72). With sunset at 18:39, solar UV exposure drops to zero for the overnight period.\n\n## Extended Outlook\n\nRain and thunder potential remain elevated through Thursday and Friday before a broader wet trend establishes into the weekend. High temperatures through Saturday will hover near 28°C / 82°F to 30°C / 86°F with overnight lows around 18°C / 64°F. Looking further ahead into Sunday through Wednesday (days 4 to 7), model confidence lowers, though ECMWF and Best Match continue to signal rain chances around 65% into midweek.\n\n## Severe Weather / Hazard Potential\n\nConvective instability is elevated this evening across multiple models. UKMO projects peak CAPE reaching 2750 J/kg around 18:00, while ECMWF indicates 1730 J/kg, Best Match shows 920 J/kg, ICON reaches 780 J/kg, and GFS indicates 750 J/kg. Thunderstorms developed in this environment bring the hazard of sudden, violent convective wind gusts far exceeding the calm background surface flow, regardless of light baseline wind forecasts.\n\n## Winam Gulf — Conditions for Boaters\n\nBoaters on Winam Gulf should prepare for evening showers and thunderstorm activity. Peak wind gusts over the water are forecast at 39 km/h (21 kt). Convective energy over the lake remains elevated into the night, creating risks of localized squalls, abrupt choppy wave action, and erratic wind gusts near active storm cells.\n\n## Detailed Discussion\n\n### Synoptic Overview\n\nAcross roughly 2,600 km, pressure is lowest toward the northeast (1009 hPa) and highest toward the southeast (1019 hPa) — an 11 hPa spread, a strong large-scale gradient. Pressure overhead is near-steady. Sampling is a nine-point ring at 12-degree spacing, so this locates a direction, not a centre or a front. Regional mean sea level pressure across Nyanza Basin observation points ranges from 1014.1 hPa to 1016.7 hPa today, weakening slightly to 1011.5–1013.7 hPa by Day+6. This regional pressure field combines with elevated boundary layer moisture and afternoon lake-land breeze convergence to drive evening convection.\n\n### Forecaster Confidence Notes\n\nModel weighting today places primary confidence on ECMWF and Best Match, both of which maintain 100% rolling 10-check rainfall accuracy at Day+0. Long-term verification findings show that GFS systematically under-forecasts peak surface winds by an average of 19.5 km/h at Day+0, while ICON displays a persistent overnight cold bias of -2.5°C. The Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) local bulletin forecasts sunny intervals for Thursday with light showers likely in a few places and highs of 28.0°C / 82°F and lows of 20.0°C / 68°F, aligning closely with numerical consensus for isolated to scattered shower coverage across Nyanza Basin.",
  "whatsapp_summary": null,
