      "narrative_markdown": "## Overview\nMuch cloudier than yesterday. Dry until evening thunderstorms. Temperatures and winds much the same through Saturday, with rain becoming more likely.\n\n## Today's Forecast\nWarm temperatures reaching a daytime peak of 30.8°C / 87°F will prevail through the early afternoon under increasing cloud cover. Showers and thunderstorms are expected in the late afternoon or early evening (16:00–18:00), carrying an 85% probability of precipitation and bringing around 4.5 mm (0.18 in) of rainfall. Winds will follow a pattern of northeasterly overnight, turning southwest by midday, with sustained speeds rising to around 15 km/h (8 kt) before peak gusts reach 41.0 km/h (22 kt) during evening convection. Solar UV exposure will reach a Very High peak index of 9.3 around midday under partial clearings. No current ground AQI data is available as all sensors are stale; the last actual reading was 145 at Dunga Beach 3 hours ago, while current model guidance estimates air quality at 69 (Moderate, US EPA scale). Overnight temperatures will settle to a low of 18.2°C / 65°F as convective activity tapers off into late night cloudiness.\n\n## Extended Outlook\nNear-term guidance through Saturday, September 19th (Day+3) maintains a wet pattern across the Nyanza Basin, with rain expected (75% probability call) and daily rainfall totals ranging between 1.4 mm and 7.4 mm across model solutions. Daytime highs will remain steady between 27.6°C / 82°F and 30.3°C / 87°F. Looking further ahead to Wednesday, September 23rd (Day+7), precipitation remains probable (60% probability call), with model totals spanning 3.7 mm (GFS) to 6.4 mm (ECMWF) and high temperatures moderating toward 25.2°C / 77°F to 30.6°C / 87°F.\n\n## Severe Weather / Hazard Potential\nConvective instability peaks in the late afternoon and early evening near 18:00, driven by significant CAPE accumulation across the guidance. A marked spread exists across models: UKMO builds extreme CAPE to 3